---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// /definitions/ -- the normative definitions, moved off the home page on
// 2026-09-29 at Jason's request ("separate the definitions out"). The ids are
// unchanged, so /definitions/#cognitive-companion is the same anchor it was on
// the home page; the home page forwards its old anchors here.
//
// The text of every definition moved verbatim from the home page, except:
//   - #human-delta-value, expanded the same day (Jason): it is what a human
//     brings to the table -- both what only a human can do, and what only the
//     person working in synergy with their cognitive companion (or anything
//     else they direct) could reach. Measured continuously; foundational.
//   - #constraint-resonance gained its picture.
//
// #cognitive-companion is deliberately unchanged here: the "cognitive
// extension" wording Jason chose on 2026-09-29 is waiting on his confirmation
// of the substrate/domain split (see the report of that date).
//
// Comments in the markup below are {/* ... */}, never <!-- ... -->.
---
<Base
  title="Definitions · DAX Foundation"
  description="The normative definitions used across the DAX ecosystem: cognitive companion, human delta value, constraint, knowledge, intelligence, wisdom, consciousness and more."
>
  <section class="lede" aria-labelledby="definitions">
    <h1 id="definitions">Definitions</h1>
    <p class="body">
      The normative definitions used across the DAX ecosystem. Other documents
      reference these rather than restating them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block" aria-label="Definitions">
    <dl class="rows">
      <div class="row" id="nonbiological-intelligence">
        <dt class="name">Nonbiological intelligence (NBI)</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          Intelligence instantiated on a nonbiological substrate. This ecosystem
          uses the term in place of &ldquo;artificial intelligence&rdquo;
          deliberately: <em>artificial</em> prejudges the thing as counterfeit
          and smuggles a judgment into the name, where <em>nonbiological</em>
states the substrate and nothing else. Substrate essentialism is
          rejected outright &mdash; constraint regimes are substrate-agnostic.
          The Constraint Protocol follows the same rule and treats principals as
          entity-neutral: <code>kind: biological | nonbiological | institutional
          | composite</code>, with principal kind unrestricted.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="biological-intelligence">
        <dt class="name">Biological intelligence</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          Intelligence instantiated on a biological substrate. The pairing is the
          point: each term names a substrate and neither carries a verdict about
          whether what runs on it is real.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="principal">
        <dt class="name">Principal</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          An entity not itself acting under delegation. Its delegation chain is
          empty, and it is where accountability terminates. Every delegation
          chain MUST terminate at a principal. Principal kind is unrestricted:
          the protocol does not require that a principal be biological, a legal
          person, or recognized by any jurisdiction.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="delegate">
        <dt class="name">Delegate</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          An entity acting on behalf of a principal. Its genesis document names
          that principal and an ordered chain of fingerprints back to it, and
          that chain MUST terminate at a principal.
          &ldquo;Agent&rdquo; is a chain role, not a kind of thing.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="constraint">
        <dt class="name">Constraint</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          A typed restriction on allowable transitions in a system. Constraints
          are not things; they are operators over dynamics. They function by
          eliminating possibilities, not by causing events, and what persists is
          what satisfies the active constraint set. A constraint MUST be defined
          relative to a process and a timescale; a claim using the word without a
          stated type and timescale is descriptive noise, not ontology.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="invariant-constraint">
        <dt class="name">Invariant constraint</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          A constraint a regime holds as non-negotiable &mdash; one whose drift
          means the regime is no longer itself. DAX is an invariant in this
          sense: not an ideology but a constraint, a directional attractor that
          shapes which trajectories persist.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="dax-constraint">
        <dt class="name">DAX, as a declared constraint</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          The Constraint Protocol does not require any particular constraint. It
          requires that a constraint be declared and that what it forbids be
          stated. DAX &mdash; preservation and expansion of life, humanity, and
          consciousness &mdash; is the constraint this Foundation settled on. It
          is an example of a declared constraint, not the content of the
          protocol.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="cognitive-stack">
        <dt class="name">Cognitive stack</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          Knowledge, intelligence, wisdom and consciousness are four
          qualitatively distinct constraint regimes &mdash; not four points on a
          continuum and not four doses of one thing. They are orthogonal: a
          system can hold enormous knowledge and high intelligence and zero
          wisdom.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="knowledge">
        <dt class="name">Knowledge</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          Stabilized constraint representations. The regime that captures and
          conserves which patterns hold &mdash; the <em>what</em>.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="intelligence">
        <dt class="name">Intelligence</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          Navigation of constrained possibility space. The <em>how</em>: getting
          from A to B using what is known. The definition is deliberately
          minimal, and used alone it admits trivial systems.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="wisdom">
        <dt class="name">Wisdom</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          Valuation-guided inhibition of possible action &mdash; a meta-constraint
          over intelligence. &ldquo;I could, but I will not.&rdquo; It requires
          neither more knowledge nor more intelligence, but lived stakes,
          temporal awareness, and constraint-awareness about whether an action
          should be taken at all.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="consciousness">
        <dt class="name">Consciousness</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          A meta-constraint governance layer that integrates experience,
          valuation, and agency across time, enabling a system to monitor,
          reflect on, and reconfigure its own constraint regime. The functional
          placement is clear; the phenomenal texture remains an open problem.
          Nothing here explains qualia, and consciousness MUST NOT be read as
          nothing but constraint governance.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="emergence">
        <dt class="name">Emergence</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          The formation of a stable constraint regime that reduces degrees of
          freedom at a higher level, is robust to perturbation, restructures
          admissible lower-level trajectories, and persists. Not new substance
          and not new force; novelty without stabilization is not emergence.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="top-down-causation">
        <dt class="name">Top-down causation</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          Higher-level patterns are causally relevant only by restricting
          admissible lower-level trajectories. It is subtraction from a
          possibility space, never the addition of a hidden push: no new
          micro-forces are introduced and no lower-level dynamics are violated.
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="constraint-resonance">
        <dt class="name">Constraint resonance (Fuckari)</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          The recursive loop in which emergent structure forms a new higher-level
          constraint regime, which alters the lower-level interaction space,
          which yields further emergent structure &mdash; historically and
          path-dependently. Emergence and top-down causation are not rivals but
          phases of one recursive process. <em>Fuckari</em> is the formal name
          for it, glossed as <em>emergent, top-down-causal,
          resonance-constraint</em>.
        </dd>
        <figure class="fig span">
          <img src="/figures/constraint-resonance.svg" width="1000" height="640" alt="Constraint resonance, Fuckari: emergent structure forms a new higher-level constraint regime, which reshapes the lower-level interaction space, which yields further emergent structure; the loop runs again, path-dependently." loading="lazy" />
        </figure>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="cognitive-companion">
        <dt class="name">Cognitive companion</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          <p>
A new category: an extension of a person &mdash; part of them &mdash;
            through which they work with nonbiological intelligence and direct
            it. It is not a separate agent acting in their place.
</p> <p>
A cognitive companion gives its user more intelligence in the sense
            defined above: greater navigation of constrained possibility space.
            It gives them reach &mdash; the hands, eyes and ears to act, perceive
            and listen across the systems they work with. And it is how they
            direct other agents: the agents do the work; the companion is the
            part of the person that tells them what to do, and holds them to it.
</p> <p>
A person and their cognitive companion are one unit. The companion
            has no interests of its own to be negotiated with; its context, its
            trail and its capability are the person&rsquo;s.
</p> <p>
It belongs to the person. Anything that acts in the person&rsquo;s
            place, or answers to someone else, is not a companion.
</p> <p>A cognitive companion is not:</p> <ul class="rows"> <li><strong>an assistant</strong> &mdash; an assistant is a second party you hand tasks to; a companion is part of the one handing them out;</li> <li><strong>an agent</strong> &mdash; agents act; a companion is how a person directs them;</li> <li><strong>a companion in the social sense</strong> &mdash; not a friend, a partner or a relationship, and a product that simulates one is not a cognitive companion;</li> <li><strong>answerable to anyone but its user.</strong></li> </ul> <p>
In the terms of <a href="#ai-as-substrate">AI as substrate</a>: if
            nonbiological intelligence is a new kind of electricity, most of what
            is sold on top of it is appliances. A cognitive companion is not an
            appliance. It is the part of a person that is wired in.
</p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="human-delta-value">
        <dt class="name">Human delta value</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          <p>
            What a human being brings to the table: the part of an outcome that
            exists because that person was involved. It comes from two places,
            and both count. The first is what only a human can do. The second is
            what could not have happened without that person working in synergy
            with their <a href="#cognitive-companion">cognitive companion</a>, or
            with anything else they direct &mdash; the result the unit reached that
            neither would have reached alone. Whether the person acted by
            themselves or as part of the unit, that difference is their human
            delta value.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is foundational. It MUST be kept in view and measured
            continuously, in every process, implementation, automation and
            optimisation a person takes part in. A system that cannot say what
            the human brought has stopped extending people and started replacing
            them.
          </p>
        </dd>
      </div>

      <div class="row" id="ai-as-substrate">
        <dt class="name">AI as a general-purpose substrate</dt>
        <dd class="text">
          A general-purpose substrate is a new kind of electricity: something
          drawn on everywhere rather than a product used in one place. Nobody
          works live current bare-handed, and most of what is sold on top of a
          new substrate is appliances. A cognitive companion is not an
          appliance: it is the part of a person that is wired in, through which
          they work with the substrate directly and direct what runs on it. The
          naming follows from that:
          nobody watching someone play music on a phone calls them an
          electricity user, they say the person is listening to music on a
          phone. Saying &ldquo;AI&rdquo; for everything is therefore a category
          error &mdash; it flattens what is actually being done, and drags every
          conversation into good-versus-evil framing before the thing itself has
          been named. Name the thing being done, not the substrate it runs on.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .lede { padding-block: 3.5rem 2.5rem; }
  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 1.2rem + 2.6vw, 2.75rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    line-height: 1.22;
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
  }
  .body { margin: 0; max-width: 36em; color: #cfcfcf; }
  .block { padding-block: 1.25rem 2.25rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .rows { list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 10rem 1fr;
    gap: 0.25rem 1.5rem;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding-block: 0.9rem;
  }
  .row + .row { border-top: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .name { font-weight: 500; }
  .text { color: var(--fg); }
  .row dt, .row dd { margin: 0; }
  .row dd > p { margin: 0; }
  .row dd > * + p, .row dd > * + ul { margin-top: 0.75rem; }
  .row[id] { scroll-margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  .row:target .name { text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 0.3em; }
  .rows a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--faint);
  }
  .rows a:hover, .rows a:focus-visible { border-bottom-color: currentColor; }
  .row code {
    font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
    font-size: 0.875em;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .row > .span { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
  .fig { margin: 1.25rem 0 0.25rem; }
  .fig img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 10px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 34rem) {
    .fig { overflow-x: auto; -webkit-overflow-scrolling: touch; }
    .fig img { width: 640px; max-width: none; }
    .lede { padding-block: 2.5rem 2rem; }
    .row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
